//============================================================================
//  Pin constraints for the 16-bit SDRAM (T3).
//
//  Generated from tang/mega.cst, the board constraint file supplied for this
//  project, by renaming its signals to the ports of tang/gametank_tang.sv:
//    IO_sdram_dq -> sdram_dq, O_sdram_addr -> sdram_a, O_sdram_wen_n ->
//    sdram_we_n, and so on. No pin number was typed by hand.
//
//  *** Two things to know before trusting this on hardware. ***
//
//  1. tang/mega.cst calls itself "CST for Console 60K/138K" — the Tang
//     Console, not the Mega 138K Pro Dock this port targets. Every signal the
//     two boards share does match Sipeed's dock reference (clock P16, button
//     K16, the five LEDs, all four TMDS pairs), which is good evidence the
//     carriers assign the SoM identically, and the commented-out lines in that
//     file place the SDRAM on PMOD1. It is still evidence, not proof: confirm
//     against the dock schematic before powering a module up.
//
//  2. There is no SDRAM CKE pin in that file (only the DDR3's `ddr_cke`), so
//     the board ties clock-enable high. tang/sdram.sv still drives `sd_cke`;
//     the top simply leaves it unconnected. The controller's power-up sequence
//     does not depend on being able to lower it.
//
//  Drive strength is left at the Gowin default, matching the source file. If
//  reads are marginal on the ribbon, that and tang/sdram.sv's RD_EXTRA are the
//  knobs — raise drive last, since ringing on a 16-bit bus is its own problem.
//============================================================================

IO_LOC "sdram_clk" V23;
IO_PORT "sdram_clk" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33 PULL_MODE=NONE;
IO_LOC "sdram_cs_n" U26;
IO_PORT "sdram_cs_n" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33 PULL_MODE=NONE;
IO_LOC "sdram_ras_n" W25;
IO_PORT "sdram_ras_n" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33 PULL_MODE=NONE;
